          defaultTimeframe: String(formData.get('defaultTimeframe')),
          darkCharts: formData.get('darkCharts') === 'on',
        });
        await action(formData);
      }}
      className="max-w-md space-y-4 rounded-3xl border border-slate-200 bg-white p-6 shadow-sm"
    >
      <label className="block space-y-2">
        <span className="text-sm font-medium text-slate-700">Default timeframe</span>
        <select
          name="defaultTimeframe"
          defaultValue={optimistic.defaultTimeframe}
          className="w-full rounded-xl border border-slate-300 px-3 py-2"
        >
          <option value="5m">5m</option>
          <option value="15m">15m</option>
          <option value="1h">1h</option>
          <option value="4h">4h</option>
        </select>
      </label>

      <label className="flex items-center gap-3">
        <input name="darkCharts" type="checkbox" defaultChecked={optimistic.darkCharts} />
        <span className="text-sm text-slate-700">Use darker chart theme</span>
      </label>

      <button disabled={pending} className="rounded-xl bg-slate-900 px-4 py-2 text-white disabled:opacity-50">
        {pending ? 'Saving...' : 'Save settings'}
      </button>

      {!state.ok && <pre className="text-sm text-red-600">{JSON.stringify(state.errors, null, 2)}</pre>}
    </form>
  );
}
```

Tradeoffs:
- Server actions are great for app-router apps, but they are not always the best fit for public APIs.
- Optimistic UI can drift briefly if the server rejects the update, so error handling still matters.

Why this choice:
- Less boilerplate than wiring separate REST endpoints for every tiny preference form.
- Validation is close to the write.
- The form feels fast and stays easy to read.

Yep. The practical Spring Boot shape now is: records for DTOs, thin controllers, focused services, and `ProblemDetail` for errors. That gives you modern Java without drowning in ceremony.

Visible reasoning:
1. Records are perfect for request/response payloads.
2. Controllers should mostly map HTTP to service calls.
3. Services are where transaction boundaries and business rules belong.
4. `ProblemDetail` gives standard JSON errors without inventing a custom error empire.

Step-by-step solution outline:
1. Define request and response records.
2. Keep the controller thin.
3. Put business logic in a service.
4. Handle domain exceptions centrally.

Code:
```java
// OrderDtos.java
package com.example.orders;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public record CreateOrderRequest(String symbol, BigDecimal qty, BigDecimal limitPrice) {}
public record OrderResponse(Long id, String symbol, BigDecimal qty, BigDecimal limitPrice, String status) {}
